In Spring Framework, when Spring creates a proxy, it uses two techniques:

1️⃣ JDK Dynamic Proxy
2️⃣ CGLIB Proxy

Spring automatically chooses one depending on your class.

1️⃣ JDK Dynamic Proxy

JDK proxy works only if the class implements an interface.

Example
public interface UserService {
    void createUser();
}
@Service
public class UserServiceImpl implements UserService {

    public void createUser() {
        System.out.println("User created");
    }
}

Spring creates a proxy like:

UserServiceProxy implements UserService

Flow:

Client
 ↓
Proxy (implements interface)
 ↓
UserServiceImpl

So the proxy implements the same interface and intercepts method calls.

Key Point

JDK proxy cannot proxy a class directly, only interfaces.

2️⃣ CGLIB Proxy

If the class does not implement any interface, Spring uses CGLIB.

CGLIB works by creating a subclass of the target class.

Example:

@Service
public class PaymentService {

    public void processPayment() {
        System.out.println("Payment processed");
    }
}

Spring generates something like:

PaymentService$$EnhancerBySpring

Flow:

Client
 ↓
CGLIB Proxy (subclass)
 ↓
PaymentService
3️⃣ Visual Difference
JDK Proxy
Interface
   ↑
Proxy implements interface
   ↑
Target class
CGLIB Proxy
Proxy extends TargetClass
4️⃣ Important Limitations
JDK Proxy

Works only with interfaces

CGLIB

Cannot proxy final classes

Cannot override final methods

Example:

public final class UserService { }

CGLIB cannot create subclass, so proxy fails.

5️⃣ Which One Spring Boot Uses?

In Spring Boot:

| Situation                  | Proxy Type        |
| -------------------------- | ----------------- |
| Class implements interface | JDK Dynamic Proxy |
| Class has no interface     | CGLIB             |

6️⃣ Interview Trick Question

If you write:

@Transactional
public void methodA() {
    methodB();
}

methodB() will not run inside proxy because internal calls bypass proxy.

This is a very common Spring interview trap.

🔑 Interview One-Line Answer

Spring creates proxies using JDK Dynamic Proxy when a bean implements an interface, and CGLIB when no interface is present,
 allowing Spring AOP features like transactions and logging to intercept method calls.